======================================================================
  3. Filtering low-count genes
======================================================================
Genes with zero counts in ALL samples: 30208 of 63677 
Kept by 'count >= 10 in >= 4 samples': 16139 
Why filter? Genes with almost no reads carry no information about DE,
 but they DO count towards multiple-testing correction and slow everything down.
 Note: DESeq2's independent filtering does this automatically at results()
 time, so this pre-filter is mainly for speed and plots.
  saved: results/02_qc/filtering_density.png

======================================================================
  4. The mean-variance relationship (why transformations matter)
======================================================================
  saved: results/02_qc/mean_variance.png
Counts are over-dispersed: variance > mean (the red Poisson line).
 This is why we model counts with the Negative Binomial distribution.
`geom_smooth()` using method = 'gam' and formula = 'y ~ s(x, bs = "cs")'
  saved: results/02_qc/meanSd_transforms.png
log2(x+1) inflates SD of low-count genes (they dominate PCA with noise).
 VST and rlog flatten that. Use VST by default; rlog when size factors vary a lot.

======================================================================
  5. PCA
======================================================================
using ntop=500 top features by variance
  saved: results/02_qc/pca_vst.png
Variance explained by PC1-4 (manual prcomp): 43.4 25.2 16.3 12 %
Look at the plot: PC1 separates treatment; PC2 is donor (N080611 sits apart).
 Within each donor, the treated sample shifts the same way along PC1.
 Donor is a strong source of variation -> put `cell` in the design (module 04).

